<div class="container-fluid py-3 py-lg-4">
    <section class="hero-gradient mb-4">
        <div class="d-flex flex-column flex-lg-row justify-content-between gap-3 align-items-lg-center">
            <div>
                <h1 class="h2 fw-bold mb-2">1.1 What is Software Architecture?</h1>
                <p class="mb-2 mb-lg-0">The strategic blueprint that shapes how software components are structured, connected, deployed, and evolved over time.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="text-lg-end">
                <span class="badge-soft">ASP.NET Core</span>
                <span class="badge-soft">Microservices Foundation</span>
                <span class="badge-soft">ItTechGenie</span>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <div class="row g-4">
        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="topic-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Introduction</h2>
                <p>Software architecture is the <strong>high-level structural vision</strong> of a software system. Before implementing controllers, services, or database logic, architects define the system boundaries, quality attributes, and communication model.</p>
                <div class="callout mt-3">
                    <strong>Why this topic matters:</strong> architecture decisions influence maintainability, scalability, cost, release speed, reliability, and even team organization.
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-lg-6">
            <div class="topic-card h-100">
                <div class="card-header p-3">Definition of Software Architecture</div>
                <div class="card-body p-3 p-lg-4">
                    <p><strong>Software architecture</strong> is the set of significant decisions about a system’s:</p>
                    <ul class="list-tight mb-3">
                        <li><span class="icon-dot"></span>Structure (layers, modules, services)</li>
                        <li><span class="icon-dot"></span>Behavior (interactions and data flow)</li>
                        <li><span class="icon-dot"></span>Technology choices (frameworks, databases, protocols)</li>
                        <li><span class="icon-dot"></span>Quality goals (security, performance, resilience)</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p class="mb-0">In short: architecture answers <em>“How will this system work as a whole?”</em></p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-lg-6">
            <div class="topic-card h-100">
                <div class="card-header p-3">Importance of Software Architecture</div>
                <div class="card-body p-3 p-lg-4">
                    <ul class="mb-3 list-tight">
                        <li><strong>Clarity:</strong> clear boundaries reduce chaos in codebases.</li>
                        <li><strong>Scalability:</strong> systems can handle growth without full rewrite.</li>
                        <li><strong>Resilience:</strong> failures are isolated and recoverable.</li>
                        <li><strong>Faster onboarding:</strong> teams understand the system quickly.</li>
                        <li><strong>Business agility:</strong> features ship with lower risk.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <div class="callout callout-success">Good architecture does not remove complexity; it <strong>organizes</strong> complexity.</div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="topic-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Key Elements of Architecture</h2>
                <div class="row g-3">
                    <div class="col-md-6 col-xl-3"><div class="p-3 border rounded-3 h-100"><strong>Components</strong><br /><small>UI, APIs, services, DB, cache</small></div></div>
                    <div class="col-md-6 col-xl-3"><div class="p-3 border rounded-3 h-100"><strong>Connectors</strong><br /><small>HTTP, gRPC, message brokers</small></div></div>
                    <div class="col-md-6 col-xl-3"><div class="p-3 border rounded-3 h-100"><strong>Constraints</strong><br /><small>Security rules, standards, compliance</small></div></div>
                    <div class="col-md-6 col-xl-3"><div class="p-3 border rounded-3 h-100"><strong>Quality Attributes</strong><br /><small>Performance, availability, maintainability</small></div></div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="topic-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Layered Architecture Diagram</h2>
<pre class="diagram-box">Users
  │
  ▼
┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Frontend / UI Layer                                           │
│ Razor Views, SPA screens, client-side validation              │
└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │
  ▼
┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ API Layer / Controllers                                       │
│ Routing, request mapping, auth checks, response contracts     │
└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │
  ▼
┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Business Layer / Services                                     │
│ Use cases, orchestration, domain rules, transactions          │
└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │
  ├────────────► External Services (Payment / Email / SMS / Maps)
  │
  ▼
┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Data Access Layer / Repositories                              │
│ EF Core, SQL queries, cache access, persistence abstractions  │
└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  │
  ▼
┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Database / Storage                                            │
│ SQL / NoSQL / Blob / Queue                                    │
└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

Architecture defines:
• where each responsibility should live
• how each layer communicates
• what engineering rules the team must follow</pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-lg-6">
            <div class="topic-card h-100 p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Types of Architecture</h2>
                <ul class="list-tight mb-0">
                    <li><strong>Monolithic:</strong> one deployable unit.</li>
                    <li><strong>Layered (N-tier):</strong> separated responsibilities by layers.</li>
                    <li><strong>Microservices:</strong> independently deployable business services.</li>
                    <li><strong>Event-Driven:</strong> asynchronous communication through events.</li>
                    <li><strong>Serverless:</strong> function-driven with managed infrastructure.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-lg-6">
            <div class="topic-card h-100 p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Monolith vs Microservices</h2>
<pre class="diagram-box">Monolith
────────
Users
  │
  ▼
┌───────────────┐
│  Single App   │  UI + Business + Data access in one deployable
└───────────────┘
  │
  ▼
┌───────────────┐
│   Single DB   │
└───────────────┘

Microservices
─────────────
Users
  │
  ▼
┌───────────────┐
│  API Gateway  │
└───────────────┘
  │        │         │
  ▼        ▼         ▼
┌────────┐ ┌───────┐ ┌──────────┐
│Product │ │Order  │ │Payment   │
│Service │ │Service│ │Service   │
└────┬───┘ └───┬───┘ └────┬─────┘
     ▼         ▼          ▼
  ProductDB  OrderDB   PaymentDB

Each service can be deployed and scaled independently.</pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="topic-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Architecture vs Design Comparison</h2>
                <div class="table-responsive">
                    <table class="table table-bordered align-middle comparison-table mb-0">
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Aspect</th>
                                <th>Architecture</th>
                                <th>Design</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Scope</td>
                                <td>System-wide structure</td>
                                <td>Component/module internals</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Primary Focus</td>
                                <td>Boundaries, communication, quality attributes</td>
                                <td>Classes, algorithms, patterns, validation flow</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Time Horizon</td>
                                <td>Long-term evolution</td>
                                <td>Feature-level implementation</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Example Decision</td>
                                <td>Use microservices + API Gateway</td>
                                <td>Apply Strategy pattern in payment processing</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="topic-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">E-commerce Real-World Architecture Flow</h2>
<pre class="diagram-box">Customer --> Web App --> API Gateway
API Gateway --> Identity Service (JWT Auth)
API Gateway --> Catalog Service --> Catalog DB
API Gateway --> Cart Service --> Redis Cache
API Gateway --> Order Service --> Order DB
Order Service --> Payment Service --> Payment Provider
Order Service --> Notification Service --> Email/SMS
Order Service --> Event Bus --> Inventory Service</pre>
                <div class="callout callout-warning mt-3">
                    Without architecture, this flow becomes tightly coupled and fragile. With architecture, each capability can scale and evolve independently.
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-lg-7">
            <div class="topic-card h-100 p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Why Architecture Matters in ASP.NET Core Microservices</h2>
                <ul class="list-tight mb-0">
                    <li>Defines service boundaries aligned to business domains.</li>
                    <li>Enables independent deployment pipelines per service.</li>
                    <li>Supports API gateway, auth, and observability patterns.</li>
                    <li>Improves fault isolation through circuit breakers and retries.</li>
                    <li>Helps teams adopt clean contracts using DTOs and versioned APIs.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-lg-5">
            <div class="topic-card h-100 p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Industry Relevance</h2>
                <p>Modern enterprises in fintech, e-commerce, healthcare, logistics, and SaaS rely on sound architecture to meet uptime SLAs, compliance rules, and continuous delivery goals.</p>
                <p class="mb-0"><strong>Interview signal:</strong> candidates who can justify architecture trade-offs are valued beyond pure coding skills.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-lg-6">
            <div class="topic-card h-100 p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Key Takeaways</h2>
                <ul class="list-tight mb-0">
                    <li>Architecture is a strategic blueprint, not just a diagram.</li>
                    <li>Early decisions have long-term impact on delivery speed and quality.</li>
                    <li>Microservices require deliberate boundaries and communication design.</li>
                    <li>Architecture and design complement each other at different levels.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-lg-6">
            <div class="topic-card h-100 p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Mini Practice Task</h2>
                <p class="mb-2">Design a high-level architecture for an <strong>online food delivery platform</strong>:</p>
                <ol class="mb-2">
                    <li>List at least 5 services (e.g., Restaurant, Order, Payment).</li>
                    <li>Define one database per service.</li>
                    <li>Add one async event flow (e.g., <em>OrderPlaced</em>).</li>
                    <li>Mention 3 quality attributes to prioritize.</li>
                </ol>
                <small class="text-muted">Timebox: 20 minutes. Present your architecture in whiteboard format.</small>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="topic-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Interview Questions (with Answers)</h2>
                <div class="accordion" id="interviewAccordion">
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="q1"><button class="accordion-button" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#a1" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="a1">1) What is software architecture?</button></h2>
                        <div id="a1" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" aria-labelledby="q1" data-bs-parent="#interviewAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">It is the high-level structural design of a system, including components, interactions, constraints, and technology choices that satisfy business and quality goals.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="q2"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#a2" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="a2">2) How is architecture different from design?</button></h2>
                        <div id="a2" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="q2" data-bs-parent="#interviewAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">Architecture addresses the system as a whole; design focuses on implementing specific components and behaviors inside that architecture.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="q3"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#a3" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="a3">3) Why is architecture critical in microservices?</button></h2>
                        <div id="a3" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="q3" data-bs-parent="#interviewAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">Microservices increase distributed complexity. Architecture defines boundaries, contracts, observability, and resilience patterns to keep that complexity manageable.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="q4"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#a4" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="a4">4) Give an example of a poor architecture decision.</button></h2>
                        <div id="a4" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="q4" data-bs-parent="#interviewAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">Sharing one database across all microservices tightly couples services and blocks independent deployment and schema evolution.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="q5"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#a5" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="a5">5) Which quality attributes should be prioritized for e-commerce?</button></h2>
                        <div id="a5" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="q5" data-bs-parent="#interviewAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">Availability, performance, security, scalability, and observability are usually top priorities for e-commerce platforms.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="topic-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Summary</h2>
                <p class="mb-0">Software architecture is the foundational blueprint that turns business goals into an organized, scalable, and resilient software system. In ASP.NET Core microservices, strong architecture is the difference between a platform that evolves confidently and one that collapses under growth.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

